    <p>
        Seventy-five of the 94 are errors, twelve are warnings and seven are info. Severity
        rates the condition. It does not say how much of your class still gets woven.
        <a href="#points">Where the point lands</a> has the codes where the two part company.
    </p>

    <chapter title="The weave class and its targets" id="weave-class">

        <p>
            The class's own shape, read before any target method is. Both javac and the weaver
            check most of it. A fault here usually stops javac first.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1001</code></td>
                <td><code>@Weave</code> names no target</td>
                <td>Name it: <code>@Weave(X.class)</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1002</code></td>
                <td>Both <code>value</code> and <code>targets</code> are set</td>
                <td>Keep one, and delete the other</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1004</code></td>
                <td>A <code>targets</code> name resolved nowhere</td>
                <td>Check it; a nested name uses <code>$</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1006</code></td>
                <td>The weave has a superclass</td>
                <td>Extend <code>Object</code>; shadow the rest</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1007</code></td>
                <td>The weave class is generic</td>
                <td>Remove the type parameters</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1008</code></td>
                <td>The weave class is not final</td>
                <td>Declare it final</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1009</code></td>
                <td>A target is named as a string</td>
                <td>Write the class literal instead</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1081</code></td>
                <td>The weave declares a constructor</td>
                <td>Initialise it from an <code>@Inject</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1082</code></td>
                <td>There is a static initialiser</td>
                <td>Remove the static initialiser</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1084</code></td>
                <td>The weave implements an interface</td>
                <td>Declare it to implement nothing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1087</code></td>
                <td>A target is itself a weave class</td>
                <td>Target what that weave targets</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1096</code></td>
                <td>The weave's own bytes are missing</td>
                <td>Give the weaver a source for them</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="A target that is absent on purpose">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1004</code> stays quiet at compile time when the declaration sets
                    <code>require = Require.OPTIONAL</code>, which is how you name a class
                    present at run time and not before it. The engine asks something else
                    entirely: whether the string is a usable binary class name at all.
                    <a href="annotations.topic">Annotations</a> has the element.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Two shapes that cannot be merged">
                <p>
                    Neither a static initialiser nor an implemented interface travels onto the
                    target, and neither report offers a remedy of its own. Take the initialiser
                    out, and declare the weave to implement nothing.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> covers what does travel.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Where the weave's own bytes come from">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1096</code> reaches a weave that merges a member, or that declares
                    a handler of its own on an instance weave, when the driver's class source
                    cannot hand its class file back. Maven wires the project's own output
                    directory in for you, so this usually means a hand-built
                    <code>ClassSource</code>; give it a byte source for the weave class with
                    <code>WeaverBuilder.weaveBytes(…)</code>. A <code>Kind.STATIC</code> weave,
                    and one whose only members are <code>@Accessor</code> or
                    <code>@Invoker</code>, never needs it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Three numbers nothing reports">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1003</code>, <code>AW1085</code> and <code>AW1086</code> are held
                    against reuse so that a later condition cannot take the number. No build
                    produces one and none of them has a remedy.
                    <a href="diagnostics.topic">Diagnostic codes</a> lists the other two.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The selector and the method it finds" id="selector">

        <p>
            The <code>method</code> string is parsed, then resolved against the target's
            declared methods. A parse failure carries an offset internally. The message
            reaching the build is the bare phrase — no caret, no offset printed with it.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1010</code></td>
                <td>The selector's owner is unknown</td>
                <td>Check the name, or drop it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1015</code></td>
                <td>The selector text does not parse</td>
                <td>Read the message; it names the fault</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1016</code></td>
                <td>Type arguments were dropped</td>
                <td>Delete them; erasure wins</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1017</code></td>
                <td>A descriptor with no <code>desc:</code> prefix</td>
                <td>Use the text it suggests</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1018</code></td>
                <td>The descriptor form is malformed</td>
                <td>Write an exact JVM descriptor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1019</code></td>
                <td>A descriptor has no return type</td>
                <td>Add it; <code>V</code> is void</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1020</code></td>
                <td>No method of the target matched</td>
                <td>Name the class that declares it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1021</code></td>
                <td>Several overloads matched</td>
                <td>Add the parameter types</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1022</code></td>
                <td>A wildcard matched several</td>
                <td>Set <code>allow</code> to the number meant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1023</code></td>
                <td>The target method is abstract</td>
                <td>Name an implementing method</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1024</code></td>
                <td>The target method is synthetic</td>
                <td>Name the method the author wrote</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1025</code></td>
                <td>The target method is native</td>
                <td>Inject into its caller instead</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="An inherited method is not a declared one">
                <p>
                    This is what <code>AW1020</code> is usually saying. Name the class that
                    declares the method, and match its erased parameter types exactly.
                    <a href="selectors.topic">Selectors</a> covers matching and
                    <a href="selector-grammar.topic">The selector grammar</a> the syntax.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A constructor selector reads as generic">
                <p>
                    The test for type arguments is the character <code>&lt;</code>, so
                    <code>method = "&lt;init&gt;()"</code> is reported as
                    <code>AW1016</code>. Change nothing. At compile time the same selector also
                    reports <code>AW1020</code>, because a constructor is not among the declared
                    methods there; the engine resolves it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="allow does not reach the engine">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1022</code> is javac's alone, and <code>allow</code> answers it. The
                    engine reports <code>AW1021</code> for the same wildcard whatever
                    <code>allow</code> says. Add the parameter types, or pin one overload with
                    the <code>desc:</code> form.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The handler" id="handler">

        <p>
            A handler's parameters are read in three bands. The target's own arguments first,
            then a <code>Callback</code>, then the <code>@Local</code> captures. Anything out of
            that order fails before the types are compared.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1005</code></td>
                <td>The handler is not static enough</td>
                <td>Declare the handler static</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1040</code></td>
                <td>The parameters are not a prefix</td>
                <td>Take the target's first arguments</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1041</code></td>
                <td>The handler returns a value</td>
                <td>Return void; use the <code>Callback</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1042</code></td>
                <td>The handler cannot be called</td>
                <td>Make it and its class public</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1050</code></td>
                <td>A <code>@Local</code> bound to nothing</td>
                <td>Pick one from the listing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1051</code></td>
                <td>Two live locals fit the type</td>
                <td>Add <code>name</code> or <code>ordinal</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1052</code></td>
                <td>The target has no variable table</td>
                <td>Recompile the target with <code>-g</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1053</code></td>
                <td><code>mutable = true</code> on a plain type</td>
                <td>Declare it <code>LocalRef&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1054</code></td>
                <td>A <code>LocalRef</code> without <code>mutable</code></td>
                <td>Add <code>mutable</code>, or the plain type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1070</code></td>
                <td>A plain <code>Callback</code> on a value</td>
                <td>Declare a <code>ReturnableCallback</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1071</code></td>
                <td>The callback's type is wrong</td>
                <td>Match the target's return type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1072</code></td>
                <td><code>value()</code> where no value exists</td>
                <td>Move it to a value-bearing point</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <note>
            <p>
                Two of the sites reporting <code>AW1005</code> ask you to declare the handler
                static and take the target as its first parameter. That compiles, and then
                refuses to weave as <code>AW1040</code>. A handler's parameters are matched
                against the target method's own arguments; the receiver is never one of them.
                Declare the weave <code>@Weave(kind = Kind.INSTANCE)</code> where the handler
                needs the instance — except a <code>@Wrap</code> handler, which
                <code>AW1005</code> refuses non-static in a weave of any kind.
            </p>
        </note>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Four sites report the one code">
                <p>
                    A static weave with a non-static handler is one of four.
                    <code>AW1005</code> also reaches a non-static <code>@Inject</code> or
                    <code>@Redirect</code> handler that the target does not own; any
                    non-static <code>@Wrap</code> handler, in a weave of any kind, with no
                    <code>Kind.INSTANCE</code> exemption; and a merged instance handler on a
                    static target method. Declaring the handler static answers all four.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Codes that reach you at one stage only">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1042</code> is javac's: the engine never checks access, so a weave
                    compiled elsewhere throws <code>IllegalAccessError</code> at the first call
                    instead. <code>AW1070</code> is the engine's, so a plain
                    <code>Callback</code> on a value-returning target compiles clean. Rule both
                    out by hand. <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and
                    callbacks</a> has the bands.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A capture with no name to bind to">
                <p>
                    Without a <code>LocalVariableTable</code> only <code>@Local(index = n)</code>
                    resolves, and <code>AW1052</code> refuses every other strategy rather than
                    guess a slot. Recompile the target with <code>-g</code>, or read the slot out
                    of its bytecode. <a href="locals.topic">Locals</a> compares the four.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A void target has no return type to match">
                <p>
                    javac reports <code>AW1071</code> when a <code>ReturnableCallback&lt;T&gt;</code>'s
                    type argument does not match the target's boxed return type, and there the
                    remedy fits: declare the matching <code>T</code>. The engine reports the same
                    code for a <code>ReturnableCallback</code> on a <b>void</b> target, which has
                    no return type to match — there the fix is to declare a plain
                    <code>Callback</code> instead.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Five branches, one code">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1050</code> covers five misses: an <code>index</code> whose slot
                    holds another type; a <code>name</code> not live at the site; an
                    <code>ordinal</code> past the candidates of its type; no live local of the
                    captured type at all; and a named variable whose declared type does not
                    match — where the fix is not another local, but declaring the parameter
                    with that variable's own type. The listing the message carries is live
                    locals of the type asked for, so the last case will not appear in it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One code, three parameter shapes">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1040</code> also reaches a <code>@Local</code> capture declared
                    before the trailing band — move it to the end, after the target's own
                    arguments and any <code>Callback</code> — and a <code>@Redirect</code> or
                    <code>@Wrap</code> handler whose parameters do not open with the operation's
                    own inputs. <a href="redirect-and-wrap.topic">Redirect and Wrap</a> has that
                    shape.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Redirect and wrap" id="redirect">

        <p>
            Both need an operation to take over. That is a call, a field access or an
            instantiation. A point that names a position instead is refused, and nothing is
            emitted for it.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1060</code></td>
                <td>Two declarations claim one call</td>
                <td>Make both <code>@Wrap</code>, or narrow one</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1061</code></td>
                <td>The point is a position, usually</td>
                <td>Point it at a call, field or <code>new</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1062</code></td>
                <td><code>Operation</code> is not last</td>
                <td>Move it to the end</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1063</code></td>
                <td>The handler takes no <code>Operation</code></td>
                <td>Add a trailing <code>Operation</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Both of the wrap codes at once">
                <p>
                    A wrap handler with no <code>Operation</code> anywhere reports
                    <code>AW1062</code> and <code>AW1063</code> together from the engine, and
                    <code>AW1063</code> alone from javac. Add the trailing <code>Operation&lt;R&gt;</code> and both go.
                    <a href="redirect-and-wrap.topic">Redirect and Wrap</a> has the handler's
                    shape.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One shape that is not yours to fix">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1061</code> also covers an instantiation whose constructor call the
                    engine could not locate. Its remedy asks you to report the class file rather
                    than work around it, and that is the right move: send it to the
                    <a href="%issues%">issue tracker</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="One call site, written two ways">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1060</code> keys a site on the text you wrote, so two declarations
                    naming one call in different words are not seen as sharing it and neither is
                    reported. Spell the target the same way in both so the collision is caught,
                    then make both <code>@Wrap</code> or narrow one — <code>priority</code> only
                    orders who writes first; it does not stop both from firing.
                    <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> covers the tie.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Members on the target" id="members">

        <p>
            <code>@Shadow</code> promises a member the target already has. Every other member
            is added to it. Both kinds are checked by javac and again while merging.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1030</code></td>
                <td>The target has no such field</td>
                <td>Check the name, and the version</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1031</code></td>
                <td>The shadowed type is wrong</td>
                <td>Declare the target's own type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1032</code></td>
                <td>A <code>@Shadow</code> field has a value</td>
                <td>Delete it; the target's wins</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1033</code></td>
                <td><code>mutable</code> clears the target's <code>final</code></td>
                <td>Drop it if the weave only reads</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1034</code></td>
                <td>Another weave adds that member</td>
                <td>Raise that weave's priority</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1080</code></td>
                <td>A merged member already exists</td>
                <td>Mark them all <code>@Unique</code>, or rename</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1083</code></td>
                <td>A merge overrides a reserved name</td>
                <td>Check that it is meant</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1088</code></td>
                <td>An instance field into a record</td>
                <td>Declare it static, or drop it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1089</code></td>
                <td>An instance field into an enum</td>
                <td>Write it from an <code>@Inject</code> at <code>HEAD</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1090</code></td>
                <td><code>@Shadow</code> in a static weave (below)</td>
                <td>Declare <code>kind = Kind.INSTANCE</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1091</code></td>
                <td><code>@Unique</code> in a static weave</td>
                <td>Delete it, or use <code>Kind.INSTANCE</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1092</code></td>
                <td>The target is anonymous or local</td>
                <td>Target the class enclosing it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1093</code></td>
                <td>A merged field has a value</td>
                <td>Set it from an <code>@Inject</code> at <code>HEAD</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1094</code></td>
                <td>A <code>@Unique</code> member was renamed</td>
                <td>Nothing; <code>silent = true</code> quiets it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1095</code></td>
                <td>A generated member already exists</td>
                <td>Rename the accessor or invoker</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1097</code></td>
                <td>An accessor writes a <code>final</code> field</td>
                <td>Use <code>@Shadow(mutable = true)</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="A static weave generates nothing">
                <p>
                    The catalogue text under <code>AW1090</code> sends a static weave to
                    <code>@Accessor</code> or <code>@Invoker</code>. The merge stage skips every
                    weave that is not <code>Kind.INSTANCE</code> first, so neither is ever
                    generated onto the target and nothing says so. Declare the weave
                    <code>@Weave(kind = Kind.INSTANCE)</code>.
                    <a href="weave-members.topic">Weave members</a> has the two kinds.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The two stages do not agree on constant">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1032</code> and <code>AW1093</code> read a field's initialiser.
                    javac reads any JLS constant variable, static or not. The engine reads only
                    the class file's <code>ConstantValue</code> attribute, which the compiler
                    writes for a <code>static final</code> field alone. An instance
                    <code>final int x = 5</code> is <code>AW1093</code> at compile time and
                    <code>AW1081</code> at weave time, because there it compiled into the
                    constructor instead. Assign such a field from a handler either way.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Marking some of them @Unique does not help">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1080</code> is excused only where every claimant on the name carries
                    <code>@Unique</code>. A mangled member and a plainly named one still collide
                    on the plain name, so mark all of them or rename all but one.
                    <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> covers the mangled spelling.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Four signatures, not one">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1083</code> matches the whole erased signature against four:
                    <code>toString()</code>, <code>equals(Object)</code>,
                    <code>hashCode()</code> and <code>main(String[])</code>. Nothing else
                    triggers it.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A mis-shaped accessor, not a mismatch">
                <p>
                    Besides a <code>@Shadow</code> of the wrong type, <code>AW1031</code> reaches
                    a generated <code>@Accessor</code> whose descriptor is neither a getter nor a
                    setter of the field it names. Nothing there is shadowed; declare it to take
                    nothing and return the field's type, or take the field's type and return
                    void.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the point lands" id="points">

        <p>
            An <code>@At</code> is resolved against the target's own instructions. Five stages
            run in order: the slice, the search, the ordinal, the shift, then a safety check.
            Any of them can refuse.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1026</code></td>
                <td><code>this</code> does not exist yet</td>
                <td>Declare the handler static</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1027</code></td>
                <td>Constructors chained with <code>this()</code></td>
                <td>Attach to the one calling super</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1043</code></td>
                <td>Fewer matches than required; below</td>
                <td>Read the listing; widen it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1044</code></td>
                <td>More matched than <code>allow</code> permits</td>
                <td>Add an ordinal or a slice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1101</code></td>
                <td>No point has that identifier</td>
                <td>Check it; nine are built in</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1102</code></td>
                <td>No shift is allowed here (below)</td>
                <td>Remove the shift</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1103</code></td>
                <td>The call is behind an <code>invokedynamic</code></td>
                <td>Inject into the method behind it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1104</code></td>
                <td>No value is produced before it</td>
                <td>Drop <code>@Result</code>, or move the point</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1105</code></td>
                <td>Site inside a <code>new</code> window; below</td>
                <td>Move it after that constructor</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1110</code></td>
                <td>The ordinal is past the matches</td>
                <td>Count from zero in the slice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1111</code></td>
                <td>The shift leaves the slice</td>
                <td>Widen the slice, or drop it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1112</code></td>
                <td><code>shift = BY</code> moves a long way</td>
                <td>Use a slice, or another point</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1120</code></td>
                <td>The slice's <code>from</code> did not resolve</td>
                <td>Check what that bound names</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1121</code></td>
                <td>The slice's <code>to</code> did not resolve</td>
                <td>Check what that bound names</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1122</code></td>
                <td>The slice ends before it begins</td>
                <td>Make <code>to</code> land at or after <code>from</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1130</code></td>
                <td>In unreachable code; below</td>
                <td>Narrow it, slice or ordinal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1131</code></td>
                <td>A catch block was cut around it</td>
                <td>Nothing; catch in the handler</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1200</code></td>
                <td>javac could not read it; below</td>
                <td>Nothing; the weaver checks it</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <warning>
            <p>
                An injection whose <code>group</code> names no declared
                <a href="annotations.topic">@Group</a> loses the group's own accounting: the
                declaration's <code>require</code> and <code>allow</code> and the group's bounds
                are never checked, so a shortfall there produces no <code>AW1043</code> and no
                <code>AW1044</code>. The point itself still reports on its own — an
                <code>@At</code> that matched nothing still raises <code>AW1043</code>, group or
                not. Check the name against the weave's own <code>@Group</code> declarations.
            </p>
        </warning>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Six ways to match too few">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1043</code> covers all six. Read the listing in the message first.
                </p>
                <list>
                    <li><p>No <code>@At</code> at all. Add one.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A point that requires a <code>target</code> and was given none. Name
                        what to match.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A point that forbids one and was given a <code>target</code>. Remove
                        it.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A point that searched the body and found nothing. Widen the selector
                        or the slice.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Fewer matches than <code>require</code>. Lower it, or correct the
                        target.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A <code>@Group</code> total outside its bounds. Check whether the
                        target changed.</p></li>
                </list>
            </def>
            <def title="A warning that also drops the site">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1105</code> is an error and drops the one site, leaving the
                    declaration's other sites woven. <code>AW1130</code> is only a warning and
                    drops its site the same way. <code>AW1104</code> is an error and abandons
                    the whole declaration. Severity alone does not say how much still wove; each
                    detail names the element by its index rather than a count.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="What @Result asks for">
                <p>
                    The summary names <code>INVOKE_AFTER</code>, and nothing checks the point.
                    The condition is positional: the instruction immediately before the site has
                    to be a call that returns a value. Drop <code>@Result</code>, or move the
                    point after such a call. <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection
                    points</a> has the nine.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Three sites, and only one asks the point">
                <p>
                    Among the nine built-in points, only <code>Point.HEAD</code> refuses a
                    shift on its own account; every other one takes it. <code>@Redirect</code>
                    and <code>@Wrap</code> refuse a shift at any point at all, because there is
                    no instruction after an operation they took over to shift onto.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> covers the nine.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A point no shipped driver can add">
                <p>
                    The remedy for <code>AW1101</code> asks for the contributing plugin on the
                    classpath. No shipped driver loads a <code>WeaverPlugin</code>, and javac
                    resolves against the nine built-in points alone. Use one of them.
                    <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a> covers what a plugin may
                    contribute.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Slice bounds, and one refusal with no code">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1120</code> and <code>AW1121</code> come from two branches: a bound
                    that matched nothing, which explains itself rather than saying what to
                    change, and a bound naming an unregistered point, which carries no remedy at
                    all. Check the bound's point and its target. A bound whose ordinal is past
                    its own matches refuses silently, and the <code>@At</code> then contributes
                    nothing.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Why no point code arrived">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1200</code> says javac could not read the target's class file,
                    usually because it is compiled in the same round as the weave. Every point
                    check for that target is skipped, and the weaver runs all of them later.
                    Nothing needs doing.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Extension methods" id="extensions">
        <secondary-label ref="experimental"/>

        <p>
            Sixteen of these seventeen are reported by javac. <code>AW1308</code> is also
            reported by the Maven plugin, off the extension index it alone builds;
            <code>AW1309</code> is reported there and nowhere else.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Code</td>
                <td>What happened</td>
                <td>What to do</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1300</code></td>
                <td>The holder class is not final</td>
                <td>Declare it final</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1301</code></td>
                <td>A public method is not static</td>
                <td>Declare it static, or private</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1302</code></td>
                <td>Nothing names a receiver</td>
                <td>Mark a parameter <code>@Receiver</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1303</code></td>
                <td><code>@Receiver</code> is on a later parameter</td>
                <td>Move it to the front</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1304</code></td>
                <td>The receiver is not a class type</td>
                <td>Name a class or an interface</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1305</code></td>
                <td>The receiver has that member</td>
                <td>Rename the extension</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1306</code></td>
                <td>The holder class is generic</td>
                <td>Remove the type parameters</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1307</code></td>
                <td>The holder has a supertype</td>
                <td>Make it a plain final class</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1308</code></td>
                <td>Two extensions make one call</td>
                <td>Rename one of them</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1309</code></td>
                <td>The receiver declares it for real</td>
                <td>Delete it, or rename it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1310</code></td>
                <td>The method is generic</td>
                <td>Use the erased type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1311</code></td>
                <td>The receiver is parameterised</td>
                <td>Name the raw type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1312</code></td>
                <td>The receiver is <code>Object</code></td>
                <td>Name the narrowest type</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1313</code></td>
                <td>Two <code>@Receiver</code> declarations</td>
                <td>Keep one of the two</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1314</code></td>
                <td>A <code>@Receiver</code> field is not constant</td>
                <td>Declare it public static final</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1315</code></td>
                <td><code>nulls</code> with no receiver value</td>
                <td>Remove <code>nulls</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>AW1316</code></td>
                <td>The first parameter is not it</td>
                <td>Take the receiver first</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Two remedies you cannot carry out">
                <p>
                    <code>AW1300</code> asks for <code>final</code>, and an <code>@Extension</code>
                    interface can never be one. <code>AW1307</code> asks the holder to extend
                    <code>Object</code> and implement nothing, which no enum, record or
                    annotation type can do. Make the holder a plain final class.
                    <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> has the shape.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="nulls is read exactly as written">
                <p>
                    Only the elements your source wrote are read back, so
                    <code>AW1315</code> fires on a value that equals the element's own default.
                    Remove <code>nulls</code>, or mark a parameter <code>@Receiver</code> to
                    contribute an instance method instead.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Where a duplicate is found, and where it is not">
                <p>
                    The agent and the testkit build no extension index, so
                    <code>AW1308</code> and <code>AW1309</code> reach you from Maven or not at
                    all — and only when the index is built, at <code>generate-sources</code>
                    for <code>stubs</code> and at <code>process-classes</code> for
                    <code>weave</code>, before either weaves a class. <code>stubs</code> prints
                    the code and the message without the remedy and never fails on it;
                    <code>weave</code> fails on the same declaration later.
                    <a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has all four.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A collision inside somebody else's jar">
                <p>
                    Both weave-time codes read manifests off the classpath, so the declaration
                    they name may belong to a dependency. Rename it in the module that declares
                    it and rebuild that module. <a href="manifest-format.topic">The weave manifest</a> has
                    the entry.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

